  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 945 AM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Widespread showers persist across southern VA \nand NE NC, before tapering off later in the day; rain lingers \ninto this evening in the southeast, along with well below normal\nhigh temperatures. \n\nOverrunning moisture to the north of developing weak low\npressure continues to bring showers to the southern 1/2 of the\narea this morning. Latest radar shows widespread rain, mostly \nlight, along and south of I-64, with scattered showers\ndiminishing to the north. There are some pockets of moderate \nrainfall across NE NC and expect this to continue there into at\nleast the early aftn. The UL trough over the east coast will \nbe slow to slide offshore later today into this evening as sfc \nhigh pressure tries to move in from the NW. Showers could \npotentially linger in the far southeast into the evening hours,\nand given current readings mostly in the 60s, lowered today's\nhighs into the lower 70s for much of southern VA and NE NC\n(which may be close to record low max values for the date).\nFarther north, highs remain below average, but will rise into\nthe upper 70s to lower 80s (warmest NE areas where some sunshine\nbreaks out later in the day). Clearing out most areas this\nevening, with lows overnight primarily in the low-mid 60s (with\nupper 60s SE). \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mainly dry with just isolated/widely scattered late day \nshowers/storms Sunday/Monday, with higher rain chances Tuesday \ninto Wednesday; strong to severe storms are possible Tuesday. \n\nNear-average temperatures return Sunday through next week; highs \nwill be in the upper 80s and low 90s. A UL trough axis sets up just \noffshore for most of the week which will put the local forecast area \nunder NW flow. Multiple shortwaves coming down the trough and a cold \nfront will lead to an overall unsettled pattern for much of the \nweek. Sunday/Sunday night should be mostly dry with the exception of \nisolated/widely scattered evening storms over the piedmont. Models \nhave trended stormier for Monday afternoon/evening, so now have \nchance PoPs for most of the FA. Being under NW flow, there is \ncertainly the possibility of a few stronger storms Monday. However, \nthe main show still looks to be the cold front Tues. With the \nstrength of trough, and even a potential sfc low <1000mb, there will \nbe the potential for severe weather, especially if the timing lines \nup with the greatest instability. The 00z ECMWF has a very \ninteresting idea for Tues into Wed with the front's parent low down \nto 995mb stacked under a closed UL low that dips as far south as PA. \nThat would be rather anomalous for July and the ECMWF is the only \nglobal model anywhere near that extreme. Will be interested to see \nhow the models trend going forward for sure. The SPC still has the \nFA highlighted in the extended severe outlook.",
